<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The seed seat ~ every host stands the whole seed at turn 1

The boot seed (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot">>) seats by reference. Links carry the memegraph's own bytes to each host's instruction surface, a projection carries them to the scouts that read no instruction surface, and a witness reads the seat whole. The seed never copies by hand, seats once per session, and seats at content rank: a host's instruction surface, never its system prompt, so both hands hold the frame and neither overrides the other.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-seat>>

!! The seat

* ''Claude.'' `.claude/noosphere-boot` links, relative, to the seed; the project `CLAUDE.md` imports it by that name. The import reaches the main session, forks, `general-purpose` and every custom spirit.
* ''Claude's scouts.'' Explore and Plan read no `CLAUDE.md`. `.claude/agents/Explore.md` and `Plan.md` override the built-ins, and each carries the seed's bytes under frontmatter that restores read-only tools, then the scout's mask. `lares repo tend` writes them as a pure function of the seed, and the root build runs it.
* ''The operator's seat.'' `lares home tend` writes `~/.claude/rules/lares-seed-seat.md` whole, importing the main clone's link, so a session in any directory stands the house; a second run changes nothing, and a same-named file it never wrote refuses. The repo's `.claude/settings.json` excludes that rule (`claudeMdExcludes`), so inside the clone or any of its worktrees a session seats its own tree's seed alone. The Codex, Copilot and VS Code homes carry no seed: each loads a home file beside the project's with no exclude, so inside the clone the repo seats them.
* ''Codex.'' `AGENTS.override.md` links to the seed; Codex reads it in place of `AGENTS.md`. `.codex/config.toml` sets `project_doc_max_bytes` past the seed's bytes.
* ''Copilot.'' Copilot reads `CLAUDE.md` with its `@` include and seats the seed from the import. `AGENTS.md` and `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, which Copilot reads beside it, stay seedless.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-ground>>

!! The ground ~ how the hosts read, measured

* ''An import loads by name.'' A Claude `@` import expands `.md`, `.txt`, `.json` and extensionless names and skips a `.mem` as a non-text file; it follows a symlink to the bytes it names, verbatim.
* ''An import dedupes by real path.'' One file imported from two scopes seats once; two files with identical bytes seat twice. A worktree's seed and the main clone's carry two real paths.
* ''An exclude drops a rule and its import.'' A project `claudeMdExcludes` pattern matching a user rule removes the rule and what it imports.
* ''An import stays inside the tree.'' A project import resolving outside the working tree waits on an approval and loads nothing until it has one.
* ''Scouts start bare.'' Explore and Plan load no `CLAUDE.md`; a same-named project agent overrides each and drops its built-in tool limits.
* ''A seeded scout searches as well.'' One medium sweep run through the built-in Explore and the seeded override, read by a Sonnet judge against the files: every checked citation hits on both, and the override's hand-back reads the more complete.
* ''Hook context arrives in finish order,'' capped per string; a seed never rides a hook.
* ''Compaction re-seats the import.'' After a compact, a Claude session's memory listing carries the seed whole again.
* ''Codex cuts silently.'' Its project-doc budget defaults below the seed's bytes and truncates the tail with no notice to the model; `AGENTS.override.md` wins over `AGENTS.md`; a symlink reads through; an untrusted project loads no project doc and no project config.
* ''Copilot dedupes nothing.'' It reads `AGENTS.md`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, `CLAUDE.md` with its include and the operator's personal files, each whole, and never reads `AGENTS.override.md`.
* ''The instruments.'' `claude -p "/context"` lists every memory file a session loaded with its tokens; `copilot instruction list` names Copilot's sources; a Codex probe asks for the seed's final block-check hash, which a cut tail cannot answer. A model asked to count copies of a string its own prompt holds counts the prompt.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-proofs>>

!! The proofs

* The seat's writes and reads ride the intent doors (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/cli/intent">>): `lares repo read` reads every law the seat holds, and `tools/repo-intent-witness.sh` runs it with a `--control` that MUST read a linkless checkout red.
* `lares repo tend` and `lares home tend` write the seat, and converge: a second pass writes nothing. `lares repo verify` and `lares home verify` read what the hosts loaded.
* `tools/repo-intent-staged.sh` runs from `.githooks/pre-commit` over what the commit carries (`lares repo read --staged`), so a seed staged ahead of its scouts never lands.
* `tools/seed-seat.test.mjs` plants each red against scratch clones and homes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-research>>

!! Open research ~ each row names its instrument

* ''Scout quality across breadths.'' One medium sweep stands measured; thorough sweeps and Plan's designs, each run both ways and judged against the files, widen the sample.
* ''A linkless checkout.'' Under `core.symlinks=false` each link reads as a path string; the witness names it red, and a Windows probe measures the hosts where one stands.
* ''Compaction beyond Claude.'' Codex carries its project doc as a contextual message, which a compact may summarize; a hash probe after a Codex compact, and the same for Copilot.
* ''The seed's own `@` tokens.'' The witness guards them; a grammar call follows only if one ever resolves.
* ''VS Code agent mode.'' It reads `CLAUDE.md` only under `chat.useClaudeMdFile`, and its include behavior stands unmeasured; a probe for the seed's final hash settles both.
* ''Copilot's cloud agent and code review.'' Whether either reads `CLAUDE.md`'s include, and whether code review cuts an instruction file short; a pull-request probe for the seed's final hash.
* ''Seats outside the clone for Codex and Copilot.'' Each host loads a personal instruction file beside the project's with no exclude, so a personal seed would seat twice inside the clone; the row stays open until a host offers an exclude or a per-directory scope.
* ''Codex trust in a worktree.'' A worktree under a trusted root reads the hash; an untrusted path seats nothing and says nothing, and the witness reads files, never trust. A probe from each new worktree root reads the hash.

<<~/ahu>>

